{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Closing out the verification caveat noted in the previous comment. The full `tests/unit` sweep finished: **6629 passed, 10 skipped, 1 error** — and the error was self-inflicted rather than a code failure. `test_code_review_gate_session.py` carries a harness guard that fails if repo HEAD moves mid-test, and a `git commit` landed while the suite was still running (HEAD moved 8f8af4b763 -> b4fed21470). Re-run on a quiescent tree: 11 passed.\n\nComplete tally for change 2589: tests/scripts 1369 passed / 3 skipped; tests/unit 6629 passed / 10 skipped; make lint exit 0 with all ratchets new=0; make typecheck Success across 750 files.\n\nWorth recording as a reusable observation, since this session hit two variants of it: test harnesses here are sensitive to concurrent activity on the same tree. That guard is doing its job — a suite run against a moving HEAD produces results that cannot be attributed to any particular commit — but it means a full sweep and a commit must not overlap. A sibling instance: a parallel agent's backup file in /tmp was overwritten by another agent on this host, invalidating a RED proof until it was redone with a worktree-local backup. Shared scratch and shared HEAD are both hazards when several agents work the same checkout family."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788463545791743001,"uuid":"c65125ea-e973-49d6-8d83-370cd03a4ad8"}